Related: #166958

## What Problem This Solves

Plugin authors cannot inspect the sibling tool calls generated in one assistant response before an individual tool executes, so they cannot reliably enforce cross-tool constraints such as standalone final submission.

## User Impact

Plugins can inspect optional `before_tool_call.toolBatch` metadata containing a host-generated response identifier, original tool-call IDs and names, and an explicit completeness state. Existing plugins keep their behavior; no plugin implementation is changed.

Early async calls receive `membership: "streaming"`, which cannot prove standalone membership. Completed responses receive the full original membership, including earlier fragments and calls rejected by validation or resolution. This preserves existing early execution; it does not promise complete future membership while the model is still generating. Native relays, direct HTTP calls, and internally dispatched child calls do not acquire this context. These public API and streaming semantics need maintainer review before this is treated as fully resolving the issue.

## Why This Change Was Made

Project membership at the response owner before execution filtering, carry it through the existing invocation scope, and expose it on the existing hook. Plugins can use the existing per-call block result; scheduling, approvals, cancellation, validation and internal whole-batch loop protection keep their current owners.

Reuse the existing hook and async execution context. Tool resolution and the event type move to their existing sibling modules to satisfy the repository's file-size limits, with the original public type export preserved. The production delta is +144/-74 lines, including these moves; the focused integration test adds 259 lines with a matching allocation for the agent-to-plugin boundary and distinct streaming, failed-sibling and isolation risks.

## Evidence

- Base: `c22f4b36a84b736aafefdd511999d63abbb3c8b7`. The initial eight new scenarios failed on this base because membership was unavailable. All ten final scenarios pass with the change.
- Real agent loop, real tool wrappers and plugin hook runner, with controlled model output: mixed calls are selectively blocked; sibling results and block feedback reach the next model request; a subsequent standalone call executes. Covers first/middle/last positions in sequential and parallel modes, invalid and unknown siblings, streamed prefixes and terminal tails, nested/direct calls, and handler isolation.
- Focused production and regression suites: 120 passing tests across five files using `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/agent-tools.tool-batch.integration.test.ts packages/agent-core/src/agent-loop.test.ts packages/agent-core/src/agent-loop.async-tools.test.ts src/plugins/hooks.before-tool-call.test.ts src/agents/tool-loop-admission.test.ts --maxWorkers=1` (final rerun after module moves: 80.85s wall time). The integration-only run passed ten tests in about 42ms test execution / 56s cold wall time including compilation; hosted CI seconds are pending.
- Targeted typecheck passed using the repository test-shard compiler settings scoped to the new integration file and its transitive production imports. Touched-file oxlint and oxfmt, Markdown lint, file-size and test-line ratchets passed. Docs link audit checked 16,224 internal links with zero broken links. Staged diff whitespace check passed.
- Live product CLI + real DeepSeek provider proof passed at the same reviewed head: selective mixed-call rejection, successful siblings, model consumption of both fresh result tokens, and a successful standalone retry. Full command, terminal output, hook events, and reproducible fixture are below.

## Live CLI behavior proof

**PASS** on reviewed head `23c5ede36f5164b316dc24d76b80ec30412a2119`, OpenClaw 2026.10.9, Node 24.22.1, Windows x64. Run: 2026-11-02T04:51:09.3224351Z to 2026-11-02T04:51:46.2900194Z (36.97s, including CLI startup).

This runs the actual product CLI entry point, `pnpm openclaw agent exec`, with its source-built distribution at the reviewed head, an isolated workspace/config/state directory, the OpenClaw runtime, and the real `deepseek/deepseek-flash` provider. Three external HTTP requests returned 200 with `text/event-stream`. No model, fetch, stream, agent-loop, or plugin-loader mocks were used. It does not claim a Gateway/channel run.

- The model's first response contained `lookup_data`, `submit_response`, and `read_reference`. All three hook events reported the same complete three-member original batch.
- The temporary plugin blocked only `submit_response`; its implementation never ran for that call. Both read-only siblings executed successfully, and both results plus the block feedback were persisted.
- The next model response contained only `submit_response`, with a different complete one-member batch. The policy allowed it.
- Each sibling generated a fresh random token only during its execution. The submission implementation accepted only the exact two tokens and the correct sum, and recorded `verifiedBothSiblingResults: true`. This proves the model received and used both sibling results rather than merely emitting a later tool call.
- The final model response was `BATCH_PROOF_DONE`; exit code 0, three assistant turns, four attempted tool calls, one intentional policy rejection. The CLI's local cost estimate was $0.001357968 (not a provider billing receipt).

The proof plugin is synthetic and local to the test, uses only public plugin registration/hook APIs, and is not part of the PR. No business plugin or tracked source was changed. Explicit `tools.profile: "full"` plus the three-tool allowlist is required: an initial setup invocation stopped before any model request because the default coding profile omitted these tools. The corrected live scenario passed on its first model run.

**Streaming scope:** The provider used real SSE transport, but this DeepSeek run did not exercise OpenClaw's OpenAI async-function early-execution path. All observed hook memberships were `complete`; this is not live proof of `membership: "streaming"`. Early async prefix/final-tail coverage remains the existing deterministic integration test. The public completeness/availability contract still requires a human maintainer decision.

