Related: #138528

## What Problem This Solves

When the session watchdog stops a scheduled agent turn, cleanup can replace its recorded stop reason and a valid recurring operator continuation can lose its next run.

## User Impact

Named and isolated scheduled runs retain a fixed watchdog cause in history and diagnostics. Chat alerts identify the stopped run and direct the operator to inspect automation history before retrying. Recurring tasks retain their valid source; started one-shot tasks are not replayed. Cancellation, restart, source refusal and provider rate-limit behavior remain unchanged.

This addresses the watchdog-attribution subset of #138528; immediate exec-denial classification and the historical approval trigger remain outside this PR.

## Why This Change Was Made

Carry the existing controller-owned stop fact through the existing completion owners, then let Cron's notification and scheduling owners consume its two recognized values. Fixed chat text does not disclose arbitrary provider/tool/cleanup prose. No new scheduler, schema, migration, option or public hook.

The merge repair preserves upstream's bounded configuration re-admission, consumer-specific policy and committed-conversation result. A refused re-admission is projected after the refusal replaces the prior error and before custody cancellation can abort the signal. Its regression is a main-passing compatibility control, not an additional main defect.

## Evidence

Candidate: staged tree `92213a84f327009ba714103d0bc1b09ba84b8833`, complete 20-file diff SHA-256 `6127454857608d799dcc5058a86f3b91224d348319fc82f0388f0c1a8c6eb589`, against pinned upstream `d3b77ae4921503f12676881f065be539d755887d`. Validation used this uncommitted merge candidate; the publication must preserve its exact tree. Checks on the previous PR head `ff3bfb83` are historical and do not validate this merge.

- Focused owner/consumer regression: 231 distinct tests in 11 files passed. The initial 178 passed; following a small handoff simplification, 40 affected cases were rerun and 53 additional current-base scheduler/completion controls passed. These are not counted as 271 distinct tests. Node command walls: initial269.174s; supplemental140.665s. Current-head CI is pending publication and is not claimed here.
- Targeted types, formatting, test-line budget and full static/runtime import graphs passed. Direct typed lint still reports the inherited handoff max-lines violation: candidate734, exact base734, allowed700. No ignore, baseline raise, timeout relaxation or dropped assertion.
- Built-Gateway baseline and candidate ordinary-success preflights passed, including stopped-state independent SQLite verification. The baseline loses the specific cause on all3 routes after900750–900823ms; the candidate retains the fixed cause in all3 durable histories after900047–900104ms, with one Cron model/tool execution per route. Webhook exact Last error and one safe isolated chat alert were independently verified from the candidate receipt. The owner-repair follow-up passed on the same source/build with a visible synthetic answer and its observed native run ID. The first driver's stale silent-response/run-ID fixture and original exit1 remain recorded, not relabeled as green.
- Independent review: canonical Codex P0–P3 follow-up with the complete20-file patch and eight full unchanged owner/caller files returned scoped-clean, zero findings. Its last pass assessed patch plus caller context; three earlier passes contained metadata/context only. This is partitioned static review, not independent test or Gateway execution. The earlier patch-only run and its explicit context gap remain recorded.

The integration suites retain real admission, SQLite, queue custody and settlement while controlling model decisions and source fixtures. Seconds-long configuration tests protect actual re-admission/custody behavior that a projection-helper assertion cannot cover; no new test file or real timing wait was added to CI tests. The actual 15-minute product proof is a separate local execution, not a CI timer change.

