## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: new Pi sessions remain missing from the session catalog beyond its 32-second refresh window when the system clock moves backward.

## User Impact

User impact: newly created Pi sessions become discoverable after the existing refresh window despite clock corrections, without premature directory rescans after a forward correction.

## Why This Change Was Made

Measure the process-local candidate snapshot lifetime with the monotonic clock. File timestamps, catalog rows, the existing 32-second policy, and continuation eligibility keep their existing contracts.

## Evidence

Base `bc097bf6404f34be1e57cdee8bd0b2d7f59a3070` → product head `2998500fa946349057da3b47820f194ed9ab270e`. Ubuntu 24 Linux container, Node v26.12.1, built OpenClaw 2026.10.9. Network disabled at the container boundary; only isolated loopback WebSocket traffic. The published collector uses the pre-existing local test-image name; its exact image ID is sha256:39a6e6f0c5f1fc700b3eab6c19b973f8c01105e2a5efa37c264d0d8130353873. The inner Node collector is included separately and needs only the normal build and isolated HOME/state/workspace directories. At most one authenticated client is active; reconnects avoid the separate Gateway stale-while-revalidate page cache while retaining the same process-local Pi cache.

