Closes #182946

## What Problem This Solves

Voice Call cannot currently keep agent consultation available while making the delegated run's tool surface fail closed.

## User Impact

Operators can select a tool-free consultation mode for informational or policy-bounded realtime voice workflows. Existing `safe-read-only`, `owner`, and `none` behavior and defaults are unchanged.

## Why This Change Was Made

This adds explicit `realtime.toolPolicy: "consult-no-tools"` across the shared policy type and Voice Call manifest. In this mode:

- the provider can call `openclaw_agent_consult`;
- configured/custom realtime function tools are omitted;
- the existing `openclaw_end_call` lifecycle control remains available on function-tool providers; native-delegation providers reject this mode because hang-up requires their call-scoped delegated tool;
- the embedded agent receives `toolsAllow: []` and `disableTools: true`;
- routed agent workspace, sandbox session identity, and stricter authority overlays remain intact.

This is enforced during capability construction rather than relying on prompt instructions or a dummy tool.

## Evidence

Validated against `main` at `541a09d30` with Node 24.22.1:

- focused consult and Voice Call suites: 4 files, 123 tests passed;
- consultation, native-policy, tool-construction, and tool-surface guards: 7 files, 187 tests passed; shipped Voice Call consultation-owner proof covers initial and reused sessions through the real agent harness: custom realtime functions are suppressed, end-call remains exposed, delegated create/resume receive zero tools, attempted writes produce no tool result, and no filesystem effect occurs (3 files, 44 tests passed); extension/core test-import boundary check passed;
- core and extensions production typechecks passed;
- changed-file `oxfmt` passed;
- changed-file `oxlint`: 0 warnings, 0 errors;
- docs formatting passed (1,384 files checked); generated config documentation baseline refreshed and verified;
- staged diff privacy/secret scan found no private paths, credentials, or environment-specific material.

Remote CI is pending. No live provider or telephone test was performed. I reviewed the capability flow and understand the fail-closed behavior. The repository's requested `autoreview` skill was not available in this environment; an independent read-only review found no blocking issue or capability broadening.

Related: #167277

## What Problem This Solves

A background or internal turn can rebuild the tool set an existing conversation sees from that turn's narrower execution subset. Examples are a completion handoff, a scheduled wake or a follow-up without the human's grant. The audience and configuration are unchanged, but the declared tools change, so the native thread is replaced and history is replayed. Workshop and Automations are the clearest cases: their descriptions and schemas follow the current grant rather than the conversation.

## User Impact

Compatible continuations keep the conversation's tool presentation, so native Codex and Claude CLI conversations are not replaced only because an internal turn carried less authority. Every call still uses current authority: sender policy, delegation, hooks, ancestor ceilings and final-effect checks. A presented tool without a current executor refuses at call time.

One execution change is deliberate. A same-session follow-up that restates the conversation's own policy re-derives its tool list from that policy instead of inheriting the producer turn's list as a cap. A list stays a cap when any of these apply:
- an explicit allowlist;
- sender or group restrictions;
- delegation, scheduled, handed-off or isolated-completion runs;
- a different session or session incarnation.

A real change of audience, profile, role configuration, schema or runtime still invalidates the saved presentation. A new human Gateway request replaces it. A fresh human channel message clears it, while unaddressed group room events leave it unchanged. Presentation staleness fences preparation only: it never aborts an admitted run or its tool calls. This does not promise that every continuation reuses a native thread.

## Why This Change Was Made

Presentation belongs to the durable conversation owner, and executors belong to the current turn. Gateway human ingress (`chat.send` and `agent`) saves the conversation's tool-discovery presentation under the session owner. This covers the Workshop target and profile shape, and the ordinary or management Automations variant. Embedded, worker and CLI preparation reconstruct it, instead of re-deriving it from the current execution subset.

- Same-session event preparation recognizes a producer list that restates the conversation's own policy, bound to the session incarnation.
- The native CLI process-local Workshop shape cache is replaced by the durable record.
- Internal events addressed to the session's own key keep their chat type.

This adapts the same-session event approach from Marvinthebored's #184206. It does not adopt that proposal's process-local Workshop cache.

## Evidence

Head `a0a1d25ec905bfc76343d105d8623e3e48b5046c`, merged with current `main`. The live runs used `78663b9`; later commits change only paths covered by the regressions below.

- **Live, real Codex app-server and remote model.** An isolated Gateway runs session human turn → subagent completion delivered as an internal turn → human turn.

  | Build | Human turn 1 | Internal completion turn | Human turn 2 |
  | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  | `main` | thread A | new thread B: Workshop changes from personal plus workspace to workspace-only | new thread C |
  | this PR | thread A | same thread A, identical declared-tool digest | same thread A |

  Each run recalls the child result.
- **Upgrade from the published 2026.10.9.** 2026.10.9 shows the same defect: its completion turn replaces the parent thread. After `openclaw doctor --fix` (schema 25 to 39) and this head:
  - the old session keeps its OpenClaw id and history;
  - the native thread is replaced once, because the tool set differs between versions;
  - after that, the next human turn, a subagent completion and a final human turn all stay on one thread.

  The new session field is optional, and older entries load unchanged.
- **Discriminating regressions** (red on the previous code, green here):
  - unrelated profile, membership or sharing changes no longer drop presentation or abort an admitted CLI run;
  - a retried capture with stale requester facts clears instead of aborting;
  - an untargeted call from a personal presentation stays in the personal library;
  - fresh human channel turns clear saved presentation, while Gateway, internal, room, scheduled and handed-off turns do not;
  - native tool reconstruction keeps its tools after an audience change.
- **Focused suites** across discovery, session events, CLI, embedded, worker, harness, Workshop, Automations, plugin declarations and MCP pass on Windows. Twelve failures there also fail on clean `main` on Windows:
  - one compaction case in `selection.test.ts`;
  - seven `afterEach` timeouts in `skill-workshop-tool.test.ts`;
  - four `/bin/sh` cases in `prepare.completion-tools.test.ts`.
- **Static checks:**
  - `tsgo:core`, the core test type shards and `tsgo:extensions`;
  - type-aware lint, format and docs lint;
  - the line-cap, max-lines, assertion-safety and test-line ratchets (allocation equals the measured 1112 lines);
  - the config-doc baseline, which is unchanged.

  Line caps on `main` required three small sibling extractions with identical behavior.
- **Review.** Two independent review rounds. The first found two availability and isolation defects (staleness aborting runs; channel turns inheriting another requester's presentation) and five smaller issues; all are fixed with regressions. The second returned ready; its four notes are fixed or documented.

**Not covered:**
- one model per live run;
- worker and Claude CLI paths are covered by tests only;
- Gateway restart, multi-node deployment, and cache or billing effects.

**Documented limitations:**
- a revoked device or token keeps its last presentation until the next human request; execution checks still apply;
- a compaction successor starts without a saved presentation.

